comments.body:
Second, independent occurrence — confirming this still reproduces with the
current `use-m` on `unpkg.com/use-m/use.js` (unpinned), Node 24.20.0:

```bash
npx --yes node@24 --input-type=module -e "
  const code = await (await fetch('https://unpkg.com/use-m/use.js')).text();
  const { use } = await eval(code);
  const loaded = await use('command-stream');
  console.log(JSON.stringify({
    node: process.version,
    keys: Object.keys(loaded),
    dollar: typeof loaded?.\$,
    defaultDollar: typeof loaded?.default?.\$,
  }));
"
```

```json
{"node":"v24.20.0","keys":["default","module.exports"],"dollar":"undefined","defaultDollar":"function"}
```

Node 20.20.2, same command: `keys` contains the full export list and
`dollar` is `function`.

Downstream incident: `link-assistant/agent` release run 33551125228 failed with
`Error updating npm: $ is not a function` from `scripts/setup-npm.mjs`
(https://github.com/link-assistant/agent/issues/301). The same pattern exists in
eight scripts of `link-foundation/js-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template`, so
it will hit every consumer of that template on a Node 24 runner —
https://github.com/link-foundation/js-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/issues/151

Suggested fix inside `use-m`, matching the existing unwrapping rule: add
`'module.exports'` to the set of keys that are ignored when deciding whether a
namespace has "real" named exports, and prefer `ns['module.exports']` over
`ns.default` as the CommonJS value. That is exactly the Node 23+ interop marker
documented at https://nodejs.org/api/esm.html#commonjs-namespaces, so treating it
as metadata is safe on older versions too (it simply never appears there).

Consumer-side workaround until then (this is what we shipped, as
`scripts/use-module.mjs`):

```js
const METADATA_KEYS = new Set(['default', 'module.exports', '__esModule']);

export const normalizeModule = (loaded) => {
  if (loaded === null || loaded === undefined) return loaded;

  const cjsDefault = loaded['module.exports'] ?? loaded.default;
  const hasNamedExports = Object.keys(loaded).some((k) => !METADATA_KEYS.has(k));
  if (hasNamedExports || cjsDefault === undefined) return loaded;

  const type = typeof cjsDefault;
  if (type !== 'object' && type !== 'function') return loaded;

  const normalized = { default: cjsDefault };
  for (const key of Object.keys(cjsDefault)) normalized[key] = cjsDefault[key];
  return normalized;
};
```

Third downstream project, and one correction to the version boundary above.

**Incident:** [link-foundation/browser-commander#81](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/issues/81). The JS and Rust release pipelines both died on the first shell call with `TypeError: $ is not a function` ([JS run 33920348338](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/actions/runs/33920348338), [Rust run 33920348349](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/actions/runs/33920348349)), from `const { $ } = await use('command-stream')`. Same unpinned `https://unpkg.com/use-m/use.js`, same `node-version: '24.x'`.

**The boundary is Node 23, not 22.12.** The suggested regression test above says "Node 20 *and* Node ≥ 22.12"; a test pinned at 22.12 would pass and still miss this. Measured against the published `use-m`, four versions, same tree:

```
v20.20.2  32 keys  typeof $ = function   ok
v22.23.2  32 keys  typeof $ = function   ok
v23.11.1   2 keys  typeof $ = undefined  ["default","module.exports"]
v24.20.0   2 keys  typeof $ = undefined  ["default","module.exports"]
```

And without `use-m` or `command-stream` in the picture at all, so it is unambiguously the Node side — a `.cjs` file that re-exports another one defeats `cjs-module-lexer`, so Node falls back to the synthetic key:

```js
// inner.cjs
const shell = {};
shell.$ = function () {};
module.exports = shell;

// outer.cjs
module.exports = require('./inner.cjs');

// probe.mjs
const ns = await import('./outer.cjs');
console.log(process.version, JSON.stringify(Object.keys(ns)));
```

```
$ npx --yes node@22 probe.mjs   →  v22.23.2 ["default"]
$ npx --yes node@23 probe.mjs   →  v23.11.1 ["default","module.exports"]
```

Runnable version: [`experiments/ci-repro/node-module-exports-namespace-boundary.mjs`](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-81-9d4c346eb143/experiments/ci-repro/node-module-exports-namespace-boundary.mjs).

So the regression test wants a Node ≥ 23 leg. Node 22 is the LTS most consumers still run, which is exactly why this reached three projects before anyone noticed: the version that reproduces it is the one only release jobs pin.

**Fix.** Nothing to add — adding `'module.exports'` to `metadataKeys` is the right change, and it is correct for every CJS package rather than a special case, since the key is a synthetic interop marker and never a real named export. Worth noting it also fixes the `default.default` shape the workaround in link-foundation/start probes for: once the outer namespace unwraps, the inner one is the module.

**Workaround we shipped**, if a second reference is useful: [`scripts/use-module.mjs`](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-81-9d4c346eb143/scripts/use-module.mjs). It differs from the one in `link-foundation/start` in two ways that were worth the extra code:

- it *recognises the shape* (`default` plus `'module.exports'` and nothing else) rather than probing candidates for a callable member, so a package whose real export happens to be non-callable is not silently skipped;
- it traces the observed namespace keys through an opt-in logger (`CI_DEBUG=1`, off by default), so the next interop change is diagnosable from the CI log rather than from a bare `TypeError`.

All fifteen release scripts load through it, and [`use-module-adoption.test.js`](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-81-9d4c346eb143/js/tests/unit/scripts/use-module-adoption.test.js) fails if a new script calls `use(...)` directly. [`use-module-integration.test.js`](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-81-9d4c346eb143/js/tests/unit/scripts/use-module-integration.test.js) exercises the real, unpinned `use-m` on the running Node version and skips when the CDN is unreachable, so it will start passing on its own once this is released.

Agreed on the last point in the comment above, and it is the more serious half of this: an unpinned URL evaluated with `eval()` on a runner holding publish credentials means a shape change ships to every consumer's release job with no repository change and no review. Three projects hit the same failure in three days without any of them changing a line.
